<TopicPage slug="subjects/business/expert/corporate-governance">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Corporate governance is <strong>the system that keeps those who run companies accountable to
    those who own them</strong> — and to the societies that license them. It exists because of
    the agency problem: separation of ownership and control creates permanent temptation. Boards,
    audits, disclosure and incentives are the machinery; scandals are the maintenance records.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the agency problem, the board, the tools, and the stakeholder debate" widget="equationStep" config={govCfg} />

  <h2>Scandals as the field's curriculum</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Enron (2001):</strong> mark-to-market accounting abuse and special-purpose entities; collapsed a Big Five auditor (Arthur Andersen) with it. Produced Sarbanes–Oxley (2002): auditor independence, CEO certification, internal controls.</li>
    <li><strong>Wells Fargo (2016):</strong> incentive design created millions of fake accounts — governance is about the targets leadership sets, not just the controls they audit.</li>
    <li><strong>WeWork (2019):</strong> founder control (dual-class shares, 20 votes each) met reality at IPO — concentrated voting rights without accountability, priced in real time (valuation fell ~90%).</li>
    <li><strong>Activist investors and proxy fights:</strong> governance also from outside — activists force strategy changes, buybacks and board refreshes. Sometimes value-creating, sometimes short-term theatre; the evidence is mixed by design.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Jensen & Meckling, JFE (1976); Sarbanes–Oxley Act (2002); Bebchuk & Weisbach, "The State of Corporate Governance Research" (2010).</p>
</TopicPage>
